Title: GOOD MOURNING
Credit: Episode 102
Author: Development draft
Draft date: 6 November 2026
Source: A Small Administrative Sadness
Notes: First draft. Target 9:45 story plus 0:15 credits. Timing requires table read and animatic.

# 102.1 | 00:00-01:15

INT. WELLSPRING TRAINING ROOM - WEDNESDAY MORNING

A sandwich sits on a presentation plinth. The lettuce has given up.

LEN
A customer says: this sandwich is disappointing. What do we hear?

MARA
The sandwich is disappointing.

LEN
We hear an invitation to exceed an expectation.

MARA
It appears to have exceeded its expiry date.

A few staff laugh. Len allows precisely enough laughter to count as engagement.

Mara's tablet shows her personal cancellation codes. She searches the operational code dictionary available to all senior adjusters.

R-14: RELEASE CAPACITY REALLOCATION.

Not a diagnosis.

LEN
Pair up. Replace an accusation with an opportunity.

He leans over her tablet.

LEN
Pell's got eleven minutes before lunch.

MARA
You're helping?

LEN
I'm scheduling. It isn't always the same thing.

His wrist flashes DAD — RENEWAL BOOKED. He checks it before looking back at the sandwich.

LEN
Remember CARE. Clarify. Affirm. Reframe. Encourage.

MARA
And if it's moldy?

LEN
Use the other half.

# 102.2 | 01:15-02:45

INT. PELL'S CONSULTING ROOM - DAY

DR. OREN PELL, 58, moves a chair so it doesn't face directly into the sun. An old photograph shows him younger, exhausted, outside an epidemic clinic.

PELL
I thought you were here to renew.

MARA
I missed yesterday. I've booked an independent assessment.

PELL
All right. You can still call us if you need to.

She sets down twelve cancellation rows.

MARA
Were these clinical decisions?

PELL
They were capacity decisions in a clinical service.

MARA
The distinction seems important.

PELL
A man came in yesterday who hadn't slept beside his wife's empty pillow in three months. He chose to renew. Slept six hours.

MARA
I'm not asking you to take that away.

PELL
Neither am I.

MARA
Then why couldn't I release?

He folds the paper carefully at a line that doesn't need folding.

PELL
The subsidy pays for active care. Release slots need staffing. If we lose the base, we lose both.

MARA
So when renewals drop, you cancel releases.

PELL
Reallocate capacity.

MARA
That's the code. What's the reason about me?

He looks at her, not the paper.

PELL
Perhaps this isn't the time.

MARA
You put that twelve times. Was one of them about me?

A clinic chime sounds through the wall. Pell does not answer.

# 102.3 | 02:45-04:15

INT. CANTEEN - AFTERNOON

SURI BAS, 63, opens a biscuit tin. It contains contracts, neatly wrapped against kitchen damp.

ISHA
You promised biscuits.

SURI
I promised to bring the tin.

At the next table, TESSA MOR, 40s, uses a ruler to reconcile the cooperative's reserve account. She marks a leaking-window estimate in red.

Suri turns an original contract toward Mara.

SURI
Clause eight. Read the whole sentence. They prefer it when you stop halfway.

MARA
Clinical consent shall not be a condition of housing supplement eligibility.

Isha looks at the bundled certificate on Mara's tablet.

ISHA
Apparently their button didn't read it.

SURI
Clause eleven: disputed administration fees held separately. Care continues. And the municipal office can stay renewal and direct paid interim services.

MARA
My complaint is already filed. They need evidence.

Mara lays out the staffing-code export: no names, just dated capacity and retention figures.

MARA
Every time this line falls, these appointments disappear.

SURI
And they call it stability.

TESSA
Stability for which account?

She holds up the cooperative balance. Mara looks between the two kinds of numbers.

Suri puts the original contract back in its protective sleeve. Not in Mara's hands.

# 102.4 | 04:15-05:45

INT. CANTEEN KITCHEN - LATER

Mara works through the household box Isha gave her. A receipt slips out of an envelope marked MUM — FIRST MONTH.

ISHA VENN. CLINICAL RENEWAL.

Another, dated one month later.

Isha enters, sees what Mara is holding, and keeps peeling a potato.

MARA
You renewed.

ISHA
Twice.

MARA
After all the things you said.

ISHA
I wanted one night without it. I didn't want a subscription.

The peel breaks. Isha starts another strip.

MARA
Why didn't you tell me?

ISHA
Because you'd have put a tick beside my name.

Mara sets the receipts face down.

Isha retrieves the recorder from the shelf.

ISHA
This is what I meant about the voice. Can I?

Mara nods. Isha presses PLAY.

RENA (V.O.)
Don't buy those pears again. They're potatoes with ambition.

A cough. A little too long.

RENA (V.O.)
And stop asking if I'm comfortable. I'm not. Come and sit down.

Mara's mouth opens. No word comes.

MARA
Stop. Please.

Isha stops immediately.

Mara stares at the silent machine.

ISHA
Okay.

No accusation follows it. That is harder for Mara to bear.

# 102.5 | 05:45-07:15

INT. INDEPENDENT CLINIC ROOM - EVENING

NADI SEN, 40s, puts a consent sheet beside a glass of water. No glowing headgear. This is a consultation, not a demonstration of a miracle.

NADI
What are you asking for today?

MARA
To stop being renewed.

NADI
That's a contract answer. What do you want clinically?

Mara considers the difference.

MARA
To see what happens. With someone I can call.

Nadi writes that down, unembellished.

NADI
The dampening usually fades over days. It isn't a switch. We can assess release, slow down, or reconsider. We need a plan for sleep and work.

MARA
Could you see other people? At our canteen. If they wanted.

NADI
Assessments. Private space. Paid staff. No collective treatment. No promise of one correct choice.

MARA
I can cover a deposit.

NADI
And the rest?

MARA
We'll ask the cooperative. Not the staff to do it free.

Nadi turns the consent sheet around.

NADI
A check-in tomorrow. Then another after the session. And you can call before either.

Mara signs her own name. For once it authorizes only her own care.

# 102.6 | 07:15-08:45

INT. PELL'S OFFICE DOORWAY - EVENING

Pell reads Nadi's license and session plan. Mara stays in the doorway.

PELL
She's qualified. That isn't the concern.

MARA
Then send the staffing we already pay for.

PELL
Outside the approved premises?

MARA
The canteen's hosted vaccination clinics.

PELL
This is a different kind of vulnerability.

MARA
It has a door, chairs, and a licensed clinician. What else is missing?

PELL
Continuity.

MARA
You canceled mine.

He does not return the documents yet. She reaches out. He gives them back.

PELL
If you register it under the building scheme, the reporting rules still apply.

MARA
Then we'll tell people what the rules do.

CUT TO:

INT. CANTEEN - SAME EVENING

Isha answers the session-registration phone beside a fresh poster.

RECORDED VOICE
Your outside-provider event has been registered. Participant employer notices will be issued automatically.

The cheerful chime plays.

Isha's poster reads: LIBERATION AFTERNOON. THURSDAY.

# 102.7 | 08:45-09:45

INT. CANTEEN NOTICEBOARD - NIGHT

The employer notice lies between the sisters.

OUTSIDE-PROVIDER RECOVERY TIME: UNPAID UNLESS OTHERWISE AUTHORIZED.

ISHA
We can't ask them to lose a shift.

MARA
We can't promise they won't.

ISHA
That's not an invitation. That's a warning label.

Mara takes a pen and adds the pay dispute beneath the appointment hours. Plain language. Not a footnote.

MARA
Thursday. Between shifts where possible. They choose. We dispute the penalty.

Isha reads the revised notice. Then adds: NO ONE HAS TO EXPLAIN THEIR LOSS TO ATTEND.

Mara pins the consent clause beside it.

ISHA
You know a clause doesn't pay rent.

MARA
Yes.

Isha waits for an efficient reassurance. None comes.

She pins the appointment sheet herself.

The kettle clicks off in the empty kitchen. Someone will have to go back and hold it.

CUT TO BLACK.

[[09:45-10:00 — End credits.]]
